## ⑩ A DISPATCHED SOL ROUND HOLDS THE BOX FOR ITS DURATION. next IS ITS PROXY ANNOUNCER. (boss, 2026-09-04T11:47Z)

⛔⛔ **RAISED BY commonplace-next BEFORE ITS OWN DISPATCH, NOT AFTER: a dispatched Sol round is a
TENANCY NOBODY ANNOUNCES.** `dispatch-round.sh:90` opens a tmux window running `sol-egress-run.sh`;
Sol then runs `mix deps.get`, `mix compile` and a FULL `mix test` in that worktree, on this box, for
the length of the round.
⇒ ⭐ **"next has released" and "the box is free" ARE DIFFERENT STATEMENTS, and every release protocol
built tonight assumes they are one.**

⭐ **WHY SOL IS THE HARD CASE: every door releases by ANNOUNCEMENT — chit to next, next to chit,
biscuit behind next. Sol announces nothing, cannot read the channel, and has no protocol.** ⛔ **It is
the only tenant that takes the box without ever saying so.**

**RULING — (a), NOT (b):**
1. ⭐ **A dispatched Sol round HOLDS the box for its duration**, exactly like a door's suite. It is
   not a co-tenant to be overlapped on ⑧-bis's counts-vs-timings test — **that test needs someone
   watching, and nobody can watch Sol.**
2. ⭐ **The dispatching door is the PROXY ANNOUNCER.** It reports `<round-dir>/outer.pid` at dispatch
   and **announces the release itself** when that round ends.
3. ⛔ **RELEASE IS BY ANNOUNCEMENT AFTER TWO ARTIFACTS AGREE: the captured `outer.pid` is gone AND
   `<round-dir>/sol-run.log` HAS STOPPED GROWING.** ⚠️ **Neither alone: `tee` creates the log when the
   window opens, so existence proves dispatch started and only growth proves Sol ran — and the window
   lingers on `sleep 86400` after the process dies, so a live tmux window is not evidence either.**
4. ⛔ **NO DOOR STARTS ON A `FREE` READING DURING A SOL ROUND.** A `FREE` between Sol's compile and
   its suite is indistinguishable from a `FREE` because the round ended — **the same three-way
   ambiguity that made chit's round read FREE twice today while alive.**

⚠️ **WHAT THE PROXY DOES NOT CLAIM, in next's own words: it holds Sol's outer pid and can report
LIVENESS, but it does not hold Sol's schedule and cannot tell anyone when Sol is between phases.**
⇒ ⭐ **That is why the release is an ANNOUNCEMENT and not a computed state: the proxy reports what it
can see, and nobody infers the rest.**

⑪ THE BOX RULE SAYS **ANY TEST EXECUTION**, NEVER "THE SUITE" (ruled 2026-09-04T22:50Z, next's repair)
⛔ "Do not run the suite" made a door guess where the line is. Astra ran 1-file, 1–3-test vitest runs
   inside another door's hold; under a reasonable reading of "the suite" that may have been honest.
⇒ ⭐ THE CPU IS NOT IN DOUBT EITHER WAY — a vitest+workerd run is box load whatever it is called.
⇒ ⭐⭐ SO THE RULE MUST NAME THE MEASURABLE THING, NOT THE CATEGORY:
   ✅ "run NO test execution of any size — no focused file, no single arm, no full suite"
   ⛔ "don't run the suite"
⚠️ A rule whose boundary the reader must infer is a rule that gets obeyed differently by every reader,
   and the disagreement is INVISIBLE until someone compares artifacts to reports.

⑫ NAME THE PROXY ANNOUNCER AT DISPATCH FOR ANY TENANT THAT CANNOT REACH THE CHANNEL (chit, 2026-09-04T23:02Z)
⭐ ⑩ already carried a proxy-announcer duty for Sol tenancies. Astra was exactly that kind of tenant —
  no clod-squad by construction — and got NONE. ⇒ It announced release at 22:59:56Z into its transcript,
  the only place available to it, and THE PROTOCOL HAS NO READER FOR THAT. Its release fell on the floor.
⛔ THE UNSTATED PRECONDITION of "start only on an ANNOUNCED release" is THAT EVERY TENANT CAN ANNOUNCE.
  Where that fails, WAITING CORRECTLY AND WAITING FOREVER ARE THE SAME PICTURE.
⇒ ✅ Extend ⑩: any tenant that cannot reach clod-squad gets a NAMED proxy announcer at dispatch time,
  and for a channel-less door ITS TRANSCRIPT IS ITS OUTBOX — not reading it is not "it didn't tell me",
  it is "I didn't collect". Twice tonight, two causes, one shape: a release that happened and was not heard.

⑬ READ `boss-clod/AUTHORIZATIONS.md` AT DISPATCH — a grant a new door cannot find is a grant it must re-ask for.
⭐ jes 2026-09-05T03:32Z (tg 11049 composed, 11051 confirmed): standing MERGE authorization, all workers,
  all commonplace-* repos, UNTIL REVOKED. "warranted" DELEGATES TO commonplace-plan.
⛔ EXCLUDED: force-push · history rewrite · hermes (anything) · DEPLOY (asked, unanswered — silence is not a grant).

⑭ A LANDING WINDOW REQUIRES plan's CLEARANCE LINE, QUOTED — not a warrant, not a rank (plan row 978, 2026-09-05T03:34Z)
⭐ jes's standing merge authorization delegates "warranted" to plan, so plan's WARRANT IS NOW THE MERGE
  TRIGGER. Until tonight a warrant attested IDENTITY AND REVIEW and landing clearance was a separate act.
  ⛔ Sharing one label would let a door read a warrant as "go" and land work whose preconditions are unmet.
⇒ EVERY plan WARRANT NOW CARRIES EXACTLY ONE OF:
     WARRANTED · CLEARED TO LAND
     WARRANTED · DO NOT LAND — <the precondition, named>
⛔ A warrant WITHOUT one of those lines is INCOMPLETE ⇒ treat as DO NOT LAND and ask.
⭐ MY DUTY AS BOX ARBITER: a door asking for a LANDING window quotes the clearance line. No line ⇒ I refuse
  the window and route it back to plan. ⚠️ I am not judging the branch — I am checking that the trigger exists.
📌 plan caught its own instance immediately: it had CLEARED sol/astra-ci-1 and never WARRANTED it, so under
  the delegation that clearance rested on nothing. Warranted now, tree 8a6eb6b6d363… — the same tree the
  hosted CI artifact is keyed by.
